st thing I think  people need to understand is the John  Birch Society in the 1960s was the best  funded most successful and most powerful  right-wing populists  organization that the United States had  ever seen  we did populist right-wing movements  before but in this particular case the  founder of the John Birch Society a  fella named Robert Welsh actually  figured out how to get all of the  membership working on the same thing at  the same time and he used a very  monolithic approach so what he said is  what the John Birch Society did this is  not an organization that embraced the  concept of democracy farther within  itself or for the country at large my  father was the first member of the John  Birch Society in the city of Chicago and  my mother was the second they believed  so much in the cause then in 1959 they  wrote a check to the John Birch Society  for $2,000 for to live memberships that  would be like you David writing a check  for $12,500 today for membership they  were true believers my father became a  member of the National Council in 1960  when just after the National Council  which is an advisory group to Robert  Welsh just after it was founded so  interestingly enough he remained on the  leadership for 32 years this is not a  man who changed his views backtracked or  rethought his positions he remained a  true believer all of his life and even  after I left the Birch Society  he was constantly recruiting me back  into the cause so as you were growing up  I mean what at what point did you you  know for a certain time kids kind of  don't know that there are really other  options right it's just what your  parents tell you that's the status quo  it's your frame of reference period what  was the catalyst that made you start to  kind of re-evaluate where you were  politically well I think people think  this moment when I left out you know  left up and said I'm not doing this  anymore actually the story is much more  complex than that and I think it's an  object lesson for anybody who thinks  about how people live in extremism it  takes a long time it's I tell audiences  it was like one step forward to back a  half a step forward I wanted very much  to please my parents I loved my father I  loved my mother and there were very  powerful strong and compelling people so  it took probably 25 years of reading and  struggle before I was finally able to  say this is not my vision for America I  don't believe in what the John Birch  Society is preaching now the first step  forward for me had to do with meeting a  fella named Ruffalo Oliver when I was 14  years old  Rebelo Oliver was one of the John Birch  Society leaders like my dad and once my  father became a leader a whole parade of  Bergers went in and out of our life our  home in Chicago was like the stopping  point for birched leadership rebel of  Oliver's to come over quite often and he  was a fellow who was very educated a  classics professor and he had a profound  impact on my parents because Rebelo  Oliver did not believe that there was a  Holocaust during the World War two huh  so suddenly even though I'd been raised  in a Jewish neighborhood and had learned  about the Holocaust when I was a little  girl  even before the word Holocaust was  applied to the wars in Germany suddenly  my father was saying well you know the  Germans were really just trying to stop  the Communists from taking over in  Germany I thought my father and last is  night was positive rebel of Oliver and  helped him now that may not sound a  particular significance to people until  you think about this dr. rivela honor if  you google him today  you'll find out the Revel Oh Oliver is  the father of the neo-nazi movement  right it would talk to that white  supremacist last week that you were  sharing about David he would have been  very knowledgeable about rebel Oh Oliver  rebel Oh Oliver has coined the term  hallo hoax and that term is used all  across the anti-semitic of far-right  today i